One pty, many clients.

A persistent terminal is watched by every browser tab, phone and API attachment
at once, and the pty underneath has exactly one size and one acknowledgement
counter. So the pty host keeps a registry of its clients and arbitrates both
from it, rather than each caller racing to set them.

Size. Every client keeps reporting its own geometry, and the most recently
activated one wins - activation being the interactions that mean "I am looking
at this": pointer down, wheel, focus, becoming visible, a replay landing. A
phone toggling its keyboard cannot resize a desktop viewer's shell, and when the
active client leaves, the terminal falls back to the most recently active client
that still has a viewport. One that only ever reported a layout is never
promoted: it never asked for the terminal to be its size.

Flow control. Output is a broadcast but the counter is single, so the
furthest-ahead client drives it: a client that stops reading pauses its own
socket rather than the terminal everyone else is watching, and a second
attachment cannot acknowledge bytes another already did. With nobody registered
the pty drains freely - a terminal whose last reader disconnected keeps running,
and the serializer still records the output for replay.

Only what the pty itself has to arbitrate is here. The names being arbitrated
between are the ones the server channel gives out, and which client holds a
terminal, receives its output or owns its replay is that channel's own registry
(terminal-sharing.diff).

